course: dictionary
lesson: 8452
title: A word of respect
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are titles. They tell you how to show respect to a person or a holy thing. «Sahib» is one of them. It is a respectful word, like «sir» or «master», used in India and Pakistan. You will meet it in stories and history about British India.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «The Sikh holy book, the Guru Granth Sahib, is treated with honour as a living Guru.» The words treated with honour tell you the book is respected very highly, so Sahib added to a name shows great respect. Ask: what word would fit here? Then try «sir» in its place and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: In history, servants in British India used «sahib» when they spoke to British officers. That shows rank and respect, and it also reminds us that Britain ruled India and that not everyone was treated fairly. Context tells you which meaning the writer is using.
fact: Sahib is a noun, and it is a title that shows respect.
fact: «Sahib» means sir or master, a respectful word used in India and Pakistan.
fact: In the past, when Britain ruled India, servants called British officers «sahib».
fact: In Sikh use, Sahib is added to holy names as a sign of great respect, as in Guru Granth Sahib and Harmandir Sahib.
fact: «Memsahib» was a respectful word for a European woman in old India.
exercise: «The Sikh holy book, the Guru Granth Sahib, is treated with honour.» What does Sahib show here? | *Great respect | A wish to sell the book | Boredom | Surprise at the weather
exercise: Which of these is a holy name with Sahib added to it? | *Harmandir Sahib, the Golden Temple | The Sahara Desert | Big Ben | The Tower of London
exercise: In history, servants in India called British officers «sahib». What does sahib mean? | A faraway river | A kind of tent | A spice from the market | *A respectful title, like sir
test: What was «memsahib» a respectful word for? | A Sikh holy book | A servant who cooked food | A king in London | *A European woman in old India
test: Why is Sahib added to Guru Granth Sahib? | To say the book is new | To make the book heavier | *To show great respect to a holy name | To show the book is for sale
exam: Ravi reads «treated with honour» and asks what sahib shows. Which clue helps him most? | The word living, which means the book is alive and moving | *Treated with honour, which shows respect | The word Sikh, which is a town | The word book, because books are heavy
